{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Cursed are you from the ground</b> — more than it was already cursed for its sin, and in this it added to the sin that it opened its mouth, and I am adding a curse to it: it shall not again yield its strength — the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 4:11). But this is not correct, for here He did not curse the ground on his account as He did for his father, but rather He said that he would be cursed from it, and He explained the curse: that it shall not again yield its strength to him, and that he would be a fugitive and a wanderer upon it. And He said, 'When you work the ground' — for in all that you toil upon it to work it properly, in plowing and hoeing and in every field-labor, and you sow it as is fitting, it shall not again yield its strength to you, but rather you will sow much and bring in little. And this is the curse, in the sense of 'and I will curse your blessings' (Malachi 2:2). And He said this corresponding to his trade, for he was a worker of the ground, and behold his deeds were cursed. And this is the meaning of 'it shall not again yield its strength to you' — that it will no longer give you its strength as it used to do until now while you were working it, and thus Rabbi Abraham explained. And it is possible that he was cursed from the ground in that it will not give of its own self its strength to him — fig tree and vine will not yield their strength in his holding, and the tree of the field will not give him its fruit — and He turned back and said also: when you work it to plow and to sow, it shall not again yield its strength to you as in the beginning. And behold these are two curses in his trade, and the third is that he will be a fugitive and a wanderer upon it, the meaning being that his heart will not rest and he will not be at peace standing in one place upon it, but rather he will be exiled forever, for the punishment of murderers is exile. And the meaning of 'which opened its mouth' is to say: you killed your brother and covered his blood in the ground, and I will decree upon it that it shall reveal his blood and no longer cover over its slain, for you shall be punished through it and through all that it covers in it, such as sowing and planting. And this is the punishment for all bloodshed upon the earth, as it is written (Numbers 35:33), 'for blood — it profanes the earth,' and the profanation of the earth is a curse in its fruits, as in, 'when one came to a heap of twenty measures, there were but ten; when one came to the wine vat to draw fifty measures, there were but twenty' (Haggai 2:16)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:20:44.454031860Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}